issue(id="0005" status="open")
  title Distinguish prose blocks, raw blocks, and whitespace-sensitive text forms
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers formatter
    i-want text-bearing Pug constructs modeled more precisely
    so-that formatting can improve readability without breaking rendered output
  premise.
    Text handling is the most semantically dangerous area in Pug because
    whitespace can be meaningful in multiple ways. Today the formatter uses a
    broad dotted-block heuristic and preserves piped text lines largely as-is,
    while the HTML converter has a short whitelist of raw-text HTML tags. Before
    introducing line width or broader text normalization, the tool needs a
    clearer distinction between prose-like blocks, code-like raw blocks, piped
    text, and literal HTML or interpolation-heavy content.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Define explicit categories for dotted blocks, piped text, inline text, and code-like raw blocks.
    criterion Preserve blank lines, leading spaces, and trailing spaces where they are semantically significant.
    criterion Add tests that cover whitespace-control examples, interpolation-heavy prose, and raw blocks such as `script.` and `textarea.`.
    criterion Document which text forms may eventually participate in width-aware formatting and which are excluded by design.
  review-items
    item(status="open") Decide whether dotted prose blocks should be identified by tag whitelist, by syntax context, or by explicit opt-in only.
    item(status="open") Confirm whether literal HTML inside text contexts deserves its own treatment instead of falling under generic raw text handling.
    item(status="open") Keep the docs corpus examples central because they already expose whitespace-sensitive edge cases.

